(* ============================================================================
   Carve Markup Language - EBNF Grammar
   Version: 0.1

   This grammar defines the syntax of Carve, a lightweight markup language for
   documents and the web.

   Notation:
   - (*...*) = comment
   - 'x'     = terminal character
   - "x"     = terminal string
   - x | y   = alternation
   - x y     = concatenation
   - [x]     = optional (0 or 1)
   - {x}     = repetition (0 or more)
   - x+      = repetition (1 or more)
   - {x}+    = repetition (1 or more) -- same as x+, used where the repeated
               unit is a group
   - x - y   = exception (x but not y)
   - (x)     = grouping

   GUARD NOTATION (formal predicates -- machine-interpretable, PEG-class).
   These are the ONLY devices beyond pure context-free EBNF that the
   productions use. Each has exact, implementable semantics; none is prose.
   - &(p)      = positive lookahead: the input AHEAD must match p; consumes
                 nothing (PEG `&`). p may be a full production, so unbounded
                 lookahead such as "a matching closer exists ahead" is legal.
   - !(p)      = negative lookahead: the input ahead must NOT match p;
                 consumes nothing (PEG `!`).
   - <&(c) / <!(c) = single-character look-BEHIND: the character immediately
                 BEFORE the current position must (must not) match the
                 character class c; consumes nothing. Left context is always
                 exactly one already-scanned character or START, so this is a
                 regular condition, mechanically checkable.
   - x:name    = capture: bind the text matched by x to `name`.
   - where C   = semantic guard: C is a boolean expression over captures
                 using only len(name), char(name) (the run's character),
                 numeric comparison, and equality. A production carrying
                 `where` matches only when C holds. This is the single
                 counting device (fence-length matching); everything else
                 above is regular lookaround.
   - prod(d)   = production TEMPLATE: a production parameterized by a
                 terminal d, instantiated once per listed terminal (an
                 indexed-grammar macro; expansion is purely mechanical).
   - START, EOL = virtual zero-width terminals (start of inline run /
                 end of line).
   Character classes for guards: ws = space | tab | newline; alnum =
   letter | digit; punct = ascii_punctuation; non_ws = character - ws.

   NORMATIVITY
   - The files in resources/spec/*.ebnf are the NORMATIVE specification of
     Carve. resources/grammar.ebnf is their generated, byte-for-byte aggregate
     for consumers that need one file. `npm run spec:check` rejects drift.
     Where any document disagrees with those sources, the sources win. The
     specification is LAYERED: PART 0 (layout automaton)
     -> PARTS 1-8 (EBNF productions + the GUARD NOTATION above) -> PART 9
     (operational semantics for the non-context-free rules) -> PART 9R
     (two-pass resolution semantics) -> PART 10 (HTML serialization) ->
     PART 11 (the canonical writer and the other output targets) -> PART 12
     (AST serialization). PART 9 and PART 9R are the authority for behavior
     the productions cannot encode.
     `resources/spec/rules.json` gives every `-- NORMATIVE` clause a stable id.
     `target-capabilities.json` in that directory restates a sample of PART
     11's cross-target rules, one row per named clause; the clause governs.
     `layout-transitions.json` restates
     CARVE-P0-003's transition table for tooling and is INFORMATIVE: no parse
     path reads it, and the clause governs.
   - docs/case-study/syntax.md is EXPLANATORY and DERIVED: prose for
     humans, non-normative.
   - docs/spec-history.md records retired readings, implementation divergence,
     compatibility measurements and decision archaeology. Those facts may
     explain a current rule but do not belong inside its normative algorithm.
   - resources/examples/*.md and the generated tests/corpus/* pairs are the
     CONFORMANCE CONTRACT: every example is checked in CI against the spec model in `scripts/spec/` and the
     engines pinned by the conformance run. A spec change is not real until a corpus pair
     pins it.
   - resources/carve-core.ohm and scripts/spec/*.mjs are DERIVED CHECKERS.
     They execute what this file states so that a contradiction inside it
     becomes visible. They are not a fourth implementation whose behavior
     the language follows. The review and ruling policy for checkers and
     generated goldens lives under WHAT SETTLES A QUESTION in
     `.github/CONTRIBUTING.md`, and what earns a new clause lives beside it
     under WHAT EARNS A CLAUSE. Both are process rules, not language rules.

   CLI ERROR CONTRACT (NORMATIVE). A command-line invocation exits with status
   2 for an unknown option or invalid option value (`usage`), a missing or
   unreadable input file (`input-unreadable`), or invalid AST JSON supplied to
   `--from-json` (`ast-json-invalid`). The same status applies to each class
   on every implementation.

   The first stderr diagnostic line begins `carve[CATEGORY]:`, with CATEGORY
   replaced by exactly one of the three names above. The category token is
   stable for scripts; text after the colon is implementation-specific. The
   category identifies the failed input or invocation, not a parser or lint
   finding within a successfully read document.

   FORMATTER DIAGNOSTIC CONTRACT (NORMATIVE). When `carve fmt --check`
   finds a change, it prints the first changed source line for each file as
   `path:line`, followed by the old and new line text. The path for stdin is
   `<stdin>`. `carve fmt --diff` prints a unified source diff. Both modes
   leave the input unchanged and return status 1 for a formatting change,
   status 0 for no change, and the existing usage or input error status for
   failures. The diagnostic does not change what the canonical writer emits.

   RECOVERY DECISION TABLE (PARTS 0-9; construct guards still govern).
   Decide within the current host after testing the complete opener:
   | Case | Recovery |
   | Visible colon opener, optional closer | Open; close at host end, innermost first. |
   | Code/raw opener, optional closer | Open, also under a paragraph; retain payload to host end. |
   | `%%%` without exact closer ahead | Classify opener as `%%` for layout and ownership. |
   | Paired inline form without required closer | Keep declining extent literal; an unclosed backtick run consumes to block end. |
   | Invalid or incomplete opener | Use its own literal/paragraph fallback; a recognized typed prefix may retain its container and drop bad metadata. |
   Exact width, bracket extent, attribute atomicity and frontmatter shape
   retain their construct-specific tests. A failed closer search stays within
   the host; consuming an unclosed invisible comment would hide later blocks.

   ============================================================================ *)
